<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chhau dance</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chhau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chhau is a tradition of martial and folk dance from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, performed in three main styles, Purulia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, Seraikela in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> and Mayurbhanj in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. Danced mainly at the spring festival of Chaitra Parva, it enacts episodes from the epics and local legends; the Mayurbhanj style is performed without masks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed it as intangible cultural heritage in 2010.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chhau-dance.jpg" alt="Chhau dance" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suyash Dwivedi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chhau_Nritya_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
